                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b45569fd-6f78-3628-ac54-dbcd47e3bfeb",
                        "entry_id": "5ac89570-e683-3085-98fc-6580f6904587",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Dated spelling of veld."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5df5ca52-3393-40ae-b12b-8159d3dfb3e9",
                                "sense_id": "b45569fd-6f78-3628-ac54-dbcd47e3bfeb",
                                "text": "And Thane nodded and staggered blindly upward, only to sag again in a heap upon the veldt."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b9464d4d-3cc2-4bdf-bb47-a7ac30f74cdd",
                                "sense_id": "b45569fd-6f78-3628-ac54-dbcd47e3bfeb",
                                "text": "South of the Zambesi, the frontier-line separates the high veldt and the low veldt regions, the line itself sometimes running along the crest and sometimes through the middle of the broken escarpment where the high granite tablelands break down towards the sea."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aac4c8cf-bcb6-457f-84cb-f21fcbabc9b9",
                                "sense_id": "b45569fd-6f78-3628-ac54-dbcd47e3bfeb",
                                "text": "You can extend the idea of isograms to sentences that don't repeat any letters. Interestingly, there are several English sentences that use each letter exactly once. They're all pretty weird and either borrow from other languages or use acronyms that were later accepted as English words. For example:\nVeldt jynx grimps waqf zho buck\nAs I'm typing this, my word processor is marking each of the above words (save for “buck”) as spelled incorrectly, though I promise they're real words. Honest."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "32303a5f-ad12-48db-86a2-55a1b23f0eba",
                                "sense_id": "b45569fd-6f78-3628-ac54-dbcd47e3bfeb",
                                "text": "In the flatness of the veldts, one would want to communicate with someone else in order to provide theme and variation to the unbounded and unfettered thoughts stretching outward, inspired by the immense sameness of this place."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3883dfb0-1546-48ff-bafb-d81d15ae6c76",
                                "sense_id": "b45569fd-6f78-3628-ac54-dbcd47e3bfeb",
                                "text": "I forgot, in short, that I’d spent nearly five years scanning the veldt for threats, indulging in the most neurotic form of magical thinking, convinced that my monitoring of Twitter alone was what stood between Trump and national ruin, just as Erica Jong believed that her concentration and vigilance were what kept her flight from plunging into the sea."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
